{"meta":{"course":"statics","chapter":"06","section":"01","title":"6.1 Introduction to Trusses","chapter_title":"Chapter 6: Structural Analysis","description":"Understand why trusses are material-efficient, apply the two-force-member model, and use the method of joints to classify tension and compression.","learning_objectives":["Define a simple truss and identify its basic elements.","State the assumptions used in truss analysis.","Apply the Method of Joints to determine forces in truss members."],"concept_ids":["truss_analysis","method_of_joints"],"prereq_section_ids":["statics/02/03","statics/01/03"],"practice_ids":["statics_truss_intro_determinacy","statics_truss_intro_two_force","statics_truss_intro_joint_method","statics_truss_intro_sign","statics_truss_intro_diagonal_force"],"estimated_time_minutes":14},"content":"# 6.1 Introduction to Trusses\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Define** a simple truss and identify its basic elements and the two-force-member model\n2. **State** the assumptions that make classical truss analysis valid\n3. **Apply** the method of joints to determine the force in each member\n4. **Classify** each member as being in tension or compression from the sign of the result\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Bridge That Pulled Itself Apart\n\nIn August 2007 the I-35W bridge over the Mississippi River in Minneapolis collapsed during rush hour, killing 13 people. The investigation traced the failure to undersized gusset plates, the steel connectors at the joints where truss members meet. The members themselves were sound; the *joints* could not carry the axial forces the members delivered to them. Knowing the force in every member, and whether it pulls (tension) or pushes (compression), is exactly what would have flagged those connections.\n\nThat is the promise of truss analysis: convert a complicated structure and its loads into a clean list of axial member forces, each one a single number with a sign. A positive number means the member is being stretched; a negative number means it is being squeezed and might buckle. Designers size every member and every joint from that list.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** The method of joints in this section is the workhorse that produces that list of member forces, joint by joint, using nothing more than the particle equilibrium you already know.\n\n---\n\nA **truss** is a structure made from slender members connected at joints so that loads travel primarily as axial tension or compression. This makes trusses one of the most material-efficient structural systems in engineering. Bridges, roof systems, cranes, transmission towers, and aerospace frameworks all rely on the same basic idea:\n\n**use geometry to carry load with as little material as possible**\n\n## Why Trusses Are So Efficient\n\nTrusses work well because they convert complicated external loading into simpler internal member forces.\n\n- Long spans become possible without using a solid beam everywhere.\n- Members can stay slender because they mostly carry axial load.\n- Triangular layouts help preserve shape and resist collapse.\n\nThis is why a truss often feels \"lighter than it looks\" while still carrying substantial load.\n\n## The Core Assumptions\n\nClassical truss analysis depends on two simplifying assumptions:\n\n1. **Loads are applied at the joints.**\n2. **Members are connected by smooth pins.**\n\nUnder these assumptions, each member behaves as a **two-force member**. That means the internal force in the member acts along the member axis only.\n\nSo each member is typically in one of two states:\n\n- **Tension:** the member is being pulled\n- **Compression:** the member is being pushed\n\nThat is a big simplification. Instead of worrying about bending in every bar, we can focus on axial force paths.\n\n### Why a Two-Force Member Must Be Axial (a Short Derivation)\n\nThe two-force-member result is not an arbitrary rule; it follows directly from equilibrium. Isolate a single straight member and suppose the only loads on it are the two pin forces at its ends, $\\vec{F}_1$ at end 1 and $\\vec{F}_2$ at end 2 (no load is applied along its length, because all loads enter at joints).\n\nForce balance on the member requires\n\n$$\n\\sum \\vec{F} = \\vec{F}_1 + \\vec{F}_2 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\vec{F}_2 = -\\vec{F}_1 .\n$$\n\nSo the two end forces are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction. Now take moments about end 1. The moment of $\\vec{F}_1$ about end 1 is zero (it acts at that point), so\n\n$$\n\\sum M_1 = \\vec{r}_{1 \\to 2} \\times \\vec{F}_2 = 0 .\n$$\n\nA cross product is zero only when the two vectors are parallel. Therefore $\\vec{F}_2$ must be parallel to $\\vec{r}_{1 \\to 2}$, the line joining the two ends. The force is forced to lie **along the member axis**. That single fact, equal-and-opposite plus collinear, is what reduces an entire bar to one scalar: its axial force. Everything else in this section is bookkeeping built on that result.\n\n## Why Triangles Matter\n\nA four-sided frame can distort into a parallelogram unless extra bracing is added. A triangle is different. Once the side lengths are fixed, the shape is geometrically stable.\n\nThat is why trusses are built from repeated triangular units:\n\n- triangles lock geometry\n- diagonal members redirect load\n- long spans can be stabilized without filling the whole region with material\n\nThis is also why truss thinking appears so often in nature.\n\n## Bio-Inspired Link: Nature's Trusses\n\nHuman engineers did not invent lightweight lattice structures. Nature has been using them for millions of years.\n\nThe **Venus Flower Basket** sponge builds a silica skeleton with diagonal bracing and repeating lattice patterns that strongly resemble an engineered truss. The geometry helps the sponge survive deep-ocean currents while using remarkably little material.\n\nThat same logic appears in:\n\n- trabecular bone\n- leaf venation\n- lattice-like exoskeleton regions\n\nThe lesson is consistent: efficient structures do not just use strong material, they use **well-routed force paths**.\n\n## The Method of Joints\n\nOnce the overall support reactions are known, we can solve a truss joint by joint.\n\n### Procedure\n\n1. Draw a free-body diagram of the entire truss.\n2. Compute the support reactions using global equilibrium.\n3. Pick a joint with no more than two unknown member forces.\n4. Apply:\n\n$$\n\\Sigma F_x = 0, \\qquad \\Sigma F_y = 0\n$$\n\n5. Move to the next solvable joint.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Draw the FBD of the entire truss\",\"Compute support reactions from global equilibrium\",\"Pick a joint with at most two unknown members\",\"Apply sum Fx = 0 and sum Fy = 0 at that joint\",\"Move to the next solvable joint and repeat\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nA useful convention is to assume every unknown member force is in tension. If a solved value comes out negative, that member is actually in compression.\n\n### Drawing the Joint Free Body Diagram\n\nThe method of joints is just **particle equilibrium applied at a point**. Each joint is treated as a particle, and the members and external loads pulling on it are the forces. To set up a joint FBD:\n\n1. **Isolate** the joint as a dot and cut every member meeting it.\n2. **Draw** each member force along its own line, pointing **away** from the joint (the tension assumption).\n3. **Add** any external load or support reaction acting at that joint.\n4. **Resolve** each inclined member force into components using $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$ from the member geometry.\n5. **Write** $\\sum F_x = 0$ and $\\sum F_y = 0$ and solve.\n\nBecause each joint gives only two scalar equations in 2D, you can solve at most two unknown member forces per joint. That is why step 3 of the procedure insists on starting where no more than two members are unknown.\n\nFor the classic loaded joint with one inclined member at angle $\\theta$ and one horizontal member carrying a vertical load $P$ (as in Example 2), the two joint equations close to $F_{diag} = P/\\sin\\theta$ and $F_{horiz} = P/\\tan\\theta$. Drag the angle and watch how a shallow diagonal is punished: as $\\theta$ drops toward the horizontal, the diagonal force blows up far past the applied load.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Member Force vs Diagonal Angle at a Loaded Joint\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"P\",\"label\":\"Applied vertical load\",\"min\":1,\"max\":20,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"kN\",\"default\":5},{\"name\":\"theta\",\"label\":\"Diagonal angle above horizontal\",\"min\":10,\"max\":80,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"deg\",\"default\":45}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Diagonal member force P/sin(theta)\",\"unit\":\"kN\",\"expr\":\"P / sin(theta * 3.14159265 / 180)\"},{\"label\":\"Horizontal member force P/tan(theta)\",\"unit\":\"kN\",\"expr\":\"P * cos(theta * 3.14159265 / 180) / sin(theta * 3.14159265 / 180)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"theta\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"Using the explorer relation, what happens to the diagonal member force as the diagonal angle theta gets very small (the diagonal becomes nearly horizontal)?\" explanation=\"As theta approaches 0, sin(theta) approaches 0, so P/sin(theta) grows without bound. A shallow diagonal must carry a force far larger than the load it supports, which is exactly the shallow-cable lesson from Chapter 1.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It grows much larger than P because sin(theta) approaches zero.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It approaches zero because the member becomes horizontal.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It stays exactly equal to P regardless of angle.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n### Zero-Force Members\n\nA frequent and powerful shortcut: some members carry no force at all under a given loading. Two rules cover most cases.\n\n- **Two non-collinear members, no external load at the joint:** both members are zero-force members. With only two unsupported directions, equilibrium forces each to vanish.\n- **Three members where two are collinear, no external load at the joint:** the odd (non-collinear) member is a zero-force member; the two collinear members carry equal force.\n\nZero-force members are not useless. They brace other members against buckling and stiffen the truss against load cases not currently considered. Spotting them first removes unknowns and speeds up the whole solution.\n\n## Engineering Intuition\n\nWhen you solve a truss, you are really asking:\n\n- Which members are carrying the load?\n- Which members are stabilizing geometry?\n- Which members might buckle because they are in compression?\n\nThis is why truss analysis is not just algebra. It is an early form of structural design judgment.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Where do you start, and what does the sign mean?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You must solve a planar truss with eight members. Which joint do you analyze first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A joint with four unknown members meeting it\",\"next\":\"bad1\",\"feedback\":\"Each joint gives only two equations in 2D, so four unknowns cannot be solved there yet.\"},{\"label\":\"A joint with only two unknown member forces\",\"next\":\"good1\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: two equations can resolve at most two unknowns, so this joint is solvable.\"},{\"label\":\"Any joint at random; the order does not matter\",\"next\":\"bad2\",\"feedback\":\"Order matters. Picking an over-determined joint stalls the whole march.\"}]},\"good1\":{\"prompt\":\"You assume every unknown is in tension and solve a diagonal as F = -9 kN. What do you conclude?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The member is in compression and should be checked for buckling\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. A negative tensile result means compression, and compression members are buckling-critical.\"},{\"label\":\"The math failed because force cannot be negative\",\"next\":\"bad3\",\"feedback\":\"The sign is meaningful, not an error: it reports the true direction of the force.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"Resolved: start at a two-unknown joint, assume tension, and read the sign. A negative diagonal is a compression member flagged for buckling.\",\"choices\":[]},\"bad1\":{\"prompt\":\"Stuck: four unknowns, two equations. Restart and choose a solvable joint.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Go back\",\"next\":\"n1\",\"feedback\":\"Pick a joint with at most two unknowns.\"}]},\"bad2\":{\"prompt\":\"You hit an over-determined joint and cannot proceed. Restart.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Go back\",\"next\":\"n1\",\"feedback\":\"Sequence by solvability.\"}]},\"bad3\":{\"prompt\":\"Treating the sign as an error loses the buckling warning. Reconsider.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reinterpret the sign\",\"next\":\"good1\",\"feedback\":\"Negative tensile force = compression.\"}]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Roof Truss Design\" context=\"A civil engineering student must compare two roof-truss layouts for a small gymnasium and explain which members are likely to go into compression when a heavy snow load is applied.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 1: Symmetric Triangular Truss Under a Peak Load\n\n**Problem:** A simple triangle truss has a pin support at $A = (0, 0)$ and a roller at $B = (6, 0)$ m, with an apex joint $C = (3, 4)$ m. A vertical load $P = 12$ kN acts downward at $C$. Find the force in each member and classify it.\n\n**Step 1: Draw the FBD of the whole truss and find reactions.** With a symmetric geometry and a centered load, vertical equilibrium and symmetry give\n\n$$A_y = B_y = \\frac{P}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\text{ kN}, \\qquad A_x = 0$$\n\n**Step 2: Geometry of member AC.** From $A(0,0)$ to $C(3,4)$ the length is $\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5$ m, so $\\cos\\theta = 3/5 = 0.6$ and $\\sin\\theta = 4/5 = 0.8$.\n\n**Step 3: Joint A** (only two unknowns, $F_{AB}$ horizontal and $F_{AC}$ along $AC$). Assume both in tension (pulling away from the joint).\n\n$$\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad F_{AC}(0.8) + 6 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{AC} = \\boxed{-7.5 \\text{ kN (compression)}}$$\n\n$$\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad F_{AB} + F_{AC}(0.6) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{AB} = -(-7.5)(0.6) = \\boxed{+4.5 \\text{ kN (tension)}}$$\n\n**Step 4: Interpret.** The diagonal $AC$ is in compression (the negative sign flipped our tension assumption), exactly what you expect for a member propping up a downward peak load. By symmetry $F_{BC} = -7.5$ kN (compression) as well, and the bottom chord $AB$ ties the two feet together in tension. The compression diagonals are the members a designer would check for buckling.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict each step of the joint-A solution before revealing it\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"With a symmetric truss and a centered 12 kN load, what is the vertical reaction at the pin A?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"3 kN\",\"6 kN\",\"12 kN\",\"24 kN\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Symmetry splits the 12 kN load evenly: A_y = B_y = P/2 = 6 kN, and A_x = 0.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Member AC runs from (0,0) to (3,4). What is sin(theta) used in the vertical equation?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"0.6\",\"0.8\",\"1.0\",\"0.5\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Length = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5, so cos(theta) = 3/5 = 0.6 and sin(theta) = 4/5 = 0.8.\"},{\"prompt\":\"From sum F_y = 0 at joint A: F_AC(0.8) + 6 = 0. What is F_AC?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"+7.5 kN (tension)\",\"-7.5 kN (compression)\",\"-6.0 kN (compression)\",\"+4.5 kN (tension)\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"F_AC = -6 / 0.8 = -7.5 kN. The negative sign flips the tension assumption, so AC is in compression.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally sum F_x = 0: F_AB + F_AC(0.6) = 0, so F_AB = -(-7.5)(0.6) = +4.5 kN. The bottom chord AB is in tension, tying the feet together.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: A Loaded Joint with a Diagonal at 45 Degrees\n\n**Problem:** A wall bracket truss has a loaded joint $B = (3, 0)$ m connected by a horizontal member to $A = (0, 0)$ and by a diagonal member to $C = (0, 3)$ m on the wall above. A load of $5$ kN acts straight down at $B$. Find both member forces.\n\n**Step 1: Geometry.** The diagonal $BC$ runs from $B(3,0)$ to $C(0,3)$, a direction $(-3, 3)$, i.e. $45^\\circ$ above the horizontal, so $\\cos 45^\\circ = \\sin 45^\\circ = 0.7071$.\n\n**Step 2: Joint B free body diagram.** Two unknowns ($F_{BA}$ horizontal, $F_{BC}$ along the diagonal) plus the 5 kN downward load. Assume tension.\n\n$$\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad F_{BC}\\sin 45^\\circ - 5 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{BC} = \\frac{5}{0.7071} = \\boxed{+7.07 \\text{ kN (tension)}}$$\n\n$$\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad -F_{BA} - F_{BC}\\cos 45^\\circ = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{BA} = -(7.07)(0.7071) = \\boxed{-5.0 \\text{ kN (compression)}}$$\n\n**Step 3: Interpret.** The diagonal pulls up and back toward the wall to carry the load (tension); the horizontal member is pushed (compression) because the diagonal's horizontal pull would otherwise drag joint $B$ toward the wall. Notice the diagonal force (7.07 kN) exceeds the 5 kN load: a member at a shallow angle to the load must carry more than the load itself, the same lesson seen with shallow cables in Chapter 1.\n\n> **Workflow Reminder:** Start at a joint with at most two unknowns, assume every member is in tension, write $\\sum F_x = 0$ and $\\sum F_y = 0$, and read the sign. Negative means compression.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: A Five-Member Truss with a Zero-Force Member\n\n**Problem:** A roof truss has a pin support at $A = (0, 0)$ and a roller at $C = (8, 0)$ m, with a mid-span bottom joint $B = (4, 0)$ m and an apex $D = (4, 3)$ m. The five members are $AB$, $BC$ (bottom chord), $AD$, $DC$ (top chords), and $BD$ (a vertical web member). A load $P = 10$ kN acts downward at the apex $D$. Find every member force, and identify any zero-force member.\n\n**Step 1: Support reactions from global equilibrium.** Taking moments about $A$ (counterclockwise positive), the roller reaction $C_y$ acts up at $x = 8$ and the load acts down at $x = 4$:\n\n$$\n\\sum M_A = 0: \\quad C_y(8) - P(4) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; C_y = \\frac{10(4)}{8} = 5 \\text{ kN}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad A_y + C_y - P = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_y = 10 - 5 = 5 \\text{ kN}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad A_x = 0\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Member geometry.** Top chord $AD$ runs from $A(0,0)$ to $D(4,3)$, length $\\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = 5$ m, so $\\cos\\theta = 4/5 = 0.8$ and $\\sin\\theta = 3/5 = 0.6$. By symmetry $DC$ has the same angle. The web $BD$ is vertical.\n\n**Step 3: Joint A** (two unknowns: $F_{AB}$ along $+x$, $F_{AD}$ along the incline). Assume tension.\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad F_{AD}(0.6) + A_y = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{AD} = \\frac{-5}{0.6} = \\boxed{-8.33 \\text{ kN (compression)}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad F_{AB} + F_{AD}(0.8) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{AB} = -(-8.33)(0.8) = \\boxed{+6.67 \\text{ kN (tension)}}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Joint B** (members $BA$ and $BC$ horizontal, $BD$ vertical; no external load here). The vertical balance is the key step:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad F_{BD}(1) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{BD} = \\boxed{0 \\text{ kN (zero-force member)}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad -F_{BA} + F_{BC} = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{BC} = F_{BA} = +6.67 \\text{ kN (tension)}\n$$\n\nThis matches the zero-force-member rule: at joint $B$ two members are collinear ($BA$ and $BC$) and the third ($BD$) is the odd one out with no external load applied, so $BD$ carries nothing. By symmetry $F_{DC} = -8.33$ kN (compression) and the analysis is complete.\n\n**Step 5: Check at joint D.** As an independent verification, joint $D$ carries $F_{DA} = F_{DC} = -8.33$ kN (compression pushes toward $D$), $F_{DB} = 0$, and the 10 kN load down. The two top chords each push up with a vertical component $8.33(0.6) = 5.0$ kN, totaling $10.0$ kN up, which balances the load exactly. Horizontally, $8.33(0.8) = 6.67$ kN from each chord act in opposite directions and cancel. Both equations close to zero, so the solution is consistent.\n\n> **Why the Zero-Force Member Earns Its Place:** $BD$ carries no load here, but remove it and the long top chords $AD$ and $DC$ lose their midpoint brace against buckling, and any off-center or wind load would have no path through the apex. Zero-force members are insurance, not waste.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | Why Truss Force Analysis Matters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Highway and rail bridges | Each member and gusset connection is sized from its axial tension or compression; compression members are checked separately for buckling |\n| Roof and floor systems | Snow, wind, and live loads route through web and chord members; zero-force members brace long compression chords |\n| Tower cranes and booms | Lattice booms carry lifting loads as axial member forces over long, light spans |\n| Transmission and antenna towers | Wind and ice loads are resisted by triangulated lattices designed member by member |\n| Aerospace and lattice frames | Lightweight space frames trade solid material for well-routed axial load paths |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** The same joint-by-joint balance that solves a classroom triangle also sizes the members of a stadium roof and the gusset plates of a river bridge. Scale and material change; the axial-force bookkeeping does not.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Joint equilibrium (2D) | $\\sum F_x = 0,\\; \\sum F_y = 0$ |\n| Member along $(\\Delta x, \\Delta y)$, length $\\ell$ | $\\cos\\theta = \\Delta x/\\ell,\\; \\sin\\theta = \\Delta y/\\ell$ |\n| Sign rule | $F > 0 \\Rightarrow$ tension, $F < 0 \\Rightarrow$ compression (when assumed tensile) |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- A truss carries load through slender two-force members in pure tension or compression.\n- The assumptions (loads at joints, frictionless pins) make each member a two-force member.\n- Triangular units are geometrically stable; that is why trusses are built from triangles.\n- The method of joints solves one joint at a time, starting where there are at most two unknowns.\n- Assume tension everywhere; a negative result simply means the member is in compression.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next section we introduce the **method of sections**, which slices through several members at once so you can solve for a specific member force directly, without marching joint by joint across a large truss.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"truss_analysis\", \"method_of_joints\"]'></concept-map>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why is a truss member often modeled as a two-force member in introductory statics?\"\n  conceptId=\"statics_truss_basics\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because the load is assumed to enter only at the joints and the joints are idealized as pins, so the member force acts along the member axis.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because truss members can never experience either tension or compression.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because all trusses are made from weightless cables rather than solid bars.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Under the standard assumptions, each member carries only an axial force aligned with its own axis, which is why tension-compression reasoning works so well.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A student solves a member force in a truss and gets a negative value after assuming tension. What does that result mean?\"\n  conceptId=\"statics_method_of_joints\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"The member is actually in compression, opposite the original tension assumption.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"The equilibrium equations are invalid for trusses with diagonal members.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"The member carries no force because negative structural forces are impossible.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The negative sign does not mean the math failed. It means the real member force acts opposite to the assumed pulling direction, so the member is in compression.\"></interactive-quiz>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"statics_truss_intro_determinacy","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"statics_truss_basics","concept_ids":["truss_analysis"],"stem":"Why are triangular units used so heavily in truss structures?","options":["Because triangles are geometrically stable when member lengths are fixed","Because triangles eliminate all internal member forces","Because triangles guarantee every member is in tension only","Because trusses cannot include diagonal members"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Triangles resist shape change without changing member lengths, which is why they are the fundamental stable unit in trusses.","misconception_id":"triangles_only_visual_choice"},{"id":"statics_truss_intro_two_force","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"statics_truss_basics","concept_ids":["truss_analysis"],"stem":"Under the ideal truss assumptions, how does a typical member carry load?","options":["Through axial tension or compression only","Through pure bending only","Through torsion only","Through no internal force at all"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Ideal truss members are treated as two-force members, so the internal force acts along the member axis as tension or compression."},{"id":"statics_truss_intro_joint_method","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"statics_method_of_joints","concept_ids":["method_of_joints"],"stem":"Why should the method of joints usually start at a joint with two or fewer unknown member forces?","options":["Because each planar joint provides only two equilibrium equations","Because joints with three members are always unstable","Because support reactions are never needed","Because trusses cannot be analyzed from the supports"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Each planar joint gives sum Fx = 0 and sum Fy = 0, so more than two unknown member forces cannot be solved directly at that step."},{"id":"statics_truss_intro_sign","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"statics_method_of_joints","concept_ids":["method_of_joints","member_force"],"stem":"A member force is assumed to be tensile during a method-of-joints solution, but the computed value is negative. What does that mean?","options":["The truss is impossible to solve","The member is actually in compression","The member force is zero","The support reactions must be recomputed"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A negative result means the actual force direction is opposite the assumed tensile direction, so the member is in compression.","misconception_id":"negative_member_force_invalid"},{"id":"statics_truss_intro_diagonal_force","type":"numeric","difficulty":"medium","statement":"A symmetric triangular truss has a pin at A = (0, 0) m, a roller at B = (6, 0) m, and an apex at C = (3, 4) m. A 12 kN load acts straight down at C. By symmetry the support reactions are A_y = B_y = 6 kN. Using the method of joints at A, compute the MAGNITUDE of the force in diagonal member AC.","givens":{"load":"12 kN down at C","geometry":"A(0,0), B(6,0), C(3,4) m","reactions":"A_y = B_y = 6 kN"},"final_answer":{"value":7.5,"unit":"kN","tolerance":0.05},"concept_ids":["truss_analysis","method_of_joints"],"hint":"At joint A, vertical equilibrium gives F_AC * sin(theta) + A_y = 0 with sin(theta) = 4/5. The magnitude is 6 / 0.8; the negative sign indicates compression."}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"bdb5096745001bfa5cc85d685816609172d5f49b1db1788a90194d5e58c2944c","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}